-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Game Over
  Publisher   Imagine / Dinamic, 1987
  Version     1.0

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Game Over (1987)(Imagine)(128K)[re-release].z80
  MD5         e1c2ff3cfbd4f6f5a84034d57b666acf

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures are drawn on the Next's own graphics layers, so they keep their
  own colours and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is
  unchanged.

  Pace        original (-1.06% measured in JNext 1.1.104, against the original
              .z80 on a 128K machine, game state in lockstep)

  Changes     1.0: figures in their own colours on a double-buffered Layer 2,
              in the game's own plot order, over the original bitmap; scenery
              in its own attributes; the barrel's own cell colours; both
              worlds; the plot, restore and copy run whole at 28 MHz in pace
              windows; a loading screen from the same re-release's tape;
              Sinclair's 48K ROM as the whole alt ROM (NextZXOS 26.11 safe);
              the game's speed restored after every hook

  Note        Keys: on the game's menu 1 keyboard (it asks for each key), 2
              joystick, 3 play, 4 the other world; world 2 asks for the access
              code the game gives at the end of world 1. The original look:
              figures keep their own colours and the scenery keeps its own,
              each figure's ink drawn on Layer 2 in the game's own order over
              the original bitmap. The 128K release, both worlds, with its AY
              music. Fits a 1MB Next. Known issues: in JNext whole runs are
              1.1 to 1.8 percent faster than the original, but quiet scenes
              run up to 3.4 percent fast, world 2's busy scenes up to 0.6
              percent slow, and its slowest 20-update stretches reach +4.5
              percent, with one +35 percent reading not yet explained; long
              passes can take up to 3 frames longer; an occasional interrupt
              was lost (1 in about 3,750 frames). Missing: no Enhanced colour
              set yet; no cheats (the archive's POKEs do not match this
              release); natural play to the end; hardware untested.

